<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hittite Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hittites, Hittite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hittite Empire was a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, at its height in the fourteenth and thirteenth centuries BCE. It rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and destroyed the power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, and earlier sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. It has its own profile on the site and collapsed around the end of the Bronze Age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hittite-empire.jpg" alt="Hittite Empire" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nissrpska · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hittite_kingdoms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was used in correspondence between Egypt and the <b>Hittites</b> and throughout the Amarna letters of the 14th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Hittites</b> did not remain, having no interest in governing Mesopotamia, but their raid ended the First Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ramesses II and the <b>Hittites</b> made peace in 1258 BCE in the earliest treaty to survive in both parties' versions, settling a stalemate after the Battle of Kadesh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…a chance likeness of names and place the Phrygians' entry just before or after the collapse of the <b>Hittite Empire</b> at the start of the 12th century BCE, perhaps among the 'Sea Peoples'; others believe they were already settled on the Sakarya in the Late Bronze Age among…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around the 1340s BCE neighbouring kingdoms, including Mukish, Niya and Nuhashshe, attacked Ugarit when Niqmaddu II refused to join their coalition against the <b>Hittites</b>; he appealed to Suppiluliuma I, who drove the attackers off and rewarded Ugarit with territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A Mitanni treaty with the <b>Hittites</b> calls on the gods Mitra, Varuna, Indra and the Nasatyas, some of the earliest evidence for Indo-Aryan languages anywhere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
